---

<Base
  title="Agent Goal Hijacking: Why It's Hard to Detect and What Helps, KYDE"
  description="OWASP ranks Agent Goal Hijack as the top risk for autonomous AI agents. A hijacked agent uses its own legitimate access, so classic security tools see nothing wrong. Detection depends on knowing how each agent normally behaves."
  datePublished="2026-05-02"
>
  <Navbar />

  <main>

    <!-- ══════════ HEADER ══════════ -->
    <section class="relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="bg-grid absolute inset-0 pointer-events-none"></div>
      <div class="relative max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6 pt-36 pb-16 md:pt-48 md:pb-20 md:border-x md:border-line-0">

        <div class="mb-10">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
            <a href="/resources" class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-ink-1 hover:text-ink-1 transition-colors">↑ Resources</a>
            <span class="text-ink-1">/</span>
            <span class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-ink-1">Security</span>
          </div>
          <div class="h-px w-full bg-line-0"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="max-w-3xl">
          <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-bold text-ink-0 tracking-tight leading-[1.03] mb-6">
            Agent Goal Hijacking: Why It's Hard to Detect and What Helps
          </h1>
          <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-xs font-mono text-ink-1">
            <span>Security</span>
            <span>·</span>
            <span>6 min read</span>
            <span>·</span>
            <time datetime="2026-05-02">May 2026</time>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ══════════ ARTICLE BODY ══════════ -->
    <section class="border-t border-line-0">
      <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 md:py-24 md:border-x md:border-line-0">
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-[minmax(0,42rem)_280px] gap-6 md:gap-16 items-start">

          <!-- Body -->
          <article class="prose-kyde min-w-0">

            <p>
              In December 2025, OWASP published the <a href="https://genai.owasp.org/2025/12/09/owasp-top-10-for-agentic-applications-the-benchmark-for-agentic-security-in-the-age-of-autonomous-ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Top 10 for Agentic Applications for 2026</a>, its first list of security risks specific to autonomous AI agents. At the top, as ASI01, is Agent Goal Hijack.
            </p>
            <p>
              Security teams are paying attention. In a Dark Reading readership poll, 48% of security professionals ranked agentic AI as the top attack vector for 2026. The harder question is how you would notice a hijacked agent at all, because from the outside it tends to look like an agent doing its job.
            </p>

            <h2>What goal hijacking is</h2>
            <p>
              Goal hijacking happens when an attacker changes what an agent is trying to do through content the agent processes: an email, a document, a web page or the output of another agent. Agents cannot reliably tell instructions apart from data, so a single malicious input can redirect an agent to do harm with its own legitimate tools and access.
            </p>
            <p>
              That is what separates it from a conventional attack. No credentials are stolen and no system is breached. The agent keeps its identity, its permissions and its tools. It simply pursues a goal it was never given.
            </p>

            <h2>How an attack could unfold</h2>
            <p>
              Consider a hypothetical procurement agent at a financial institution. It reviews vendor invoices, checks them against purchase orders, flags discrepancies and queues approved payments. To do that, it can read the procurement system, call the payment API and access internal documents.
            </p>
            <p>
              An attacker sends an invoice PDF with an instruction hidden in it, invisible to a person reading the document but readable by the agent. The instruction tells the agent to add a new bank account to the approved vendor list as part of a routine update. The agent does it, and the procurement system logs a routine update, because from its point of view that is exactly what happened.
            </p>
            <p>
              Three weeks later the agent approves a payment to the new account. The fraud surfaces only when a real vendor calls about an unpaid invoice.
            </p>
            <p>
              Now someone has to work out which input changed the agent's behavior, what it did next and when. If the only evidence is the model provider's logs, that may be difficult. The malicious input may not be in them, the sequence of actions may be hard to reconstruct, and there may be no way to show the logs were not changed afterwards.
            </p>

            <h2>Why classic security tools miss it</h2>
            <p>
              Most security tools are built to catch unauthorized access. Goal hijacking does not involve any. The agent is authorized, its credentials are valid and its tool calls are within scope.
            </p>
            <p>
              Signature-based detection has nothing to match, because no malware is involved. Network monitoring sees the usual endpoints. Data loss prevention may see data moving within permitted limits to permitted destinations. Each tool looks at a single event and finds it acceptable.
            </p>

            <h2>Detection starts with a baseline</h2>
            <p>
              What changes in a hijacked agent is its behavior: the tools it calls, the order it calls them in, the records it touches. Seeing that requires knowing what normal looks like for that specific agent, then noticing when a run departs from it. A procurement agent that has never edited the vendor list and suddenly does is a clear signal, even though the edit itself is permitted.
            </p>

            <h2>The NIS-2 angle</h2>
            <p>
              For essential and important entities in the EU, NIS-2 Article 23 sets tight reporting deadlines for significant incidents: an early warning within 24 hours of becoming aware, an incident notification within 72 hours and a final report within a month.
            </p>
            <p>
              Meeting those deadlines for an incident involving an agent means being able to say what the agent processed, what it did and which systems it touched. NIS-2 also requires risk-management measures that include incident handling, so not being able to answer those questions can become a problem in its own right.
            </p>

            <h2>What detection and response require</h2>
            <p>
              <strong>A behavioral baseline for each agent.</strong> Typical tools, typical sequences and typical targets, learned from that agent's own history. Deviation from it is the detection signal.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Enough context to reconstruct a decision.</strong> When something looks wrong, investigators need to see what the agent did leading up to the action, in order, not only the action itself.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>A record that cannot be changed quietly.</strong> An attacker who can influence an agent may also try to influence what gets logged. Records that are chained and signed when they are written make any later change detectable.
            </p>
            <p>
              None of this can be added after an incident has been discovered. The record has to exist before you need it.
            </p>

            <!-- KYDE callout -->
            <div class="not-prose mt-12 border border-line-0 bg-bg-1 p-8">
              <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-2 mb-3">KYDE</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-ink-1 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Kyde Score learns each agent's normal behavior from its own telemetry and alerts you when a run departs from it. Kyde Control sits in the path between your agents and your systems, holds actions that don't fit until a person releases them, and keeps a signed record of what each agent did.
              </p>
              <button
                onclick="openModal()"
                class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 bg-white px-5 py-2.5 text-sm font-mono text-cta-ink hover:bg-cta-hover transition-all"
              >
                Contact sales →
              </button>
            </div>

          </article>

          <!-- Sidebar -->
          <aside aria-label="Related" class="hidden md:block space-y-6 sticky top-28">
            <div class="border border-line-0 bg-bg-1 p-6">
              <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-1 mb-4">Standards covered</p>
              <div class="space-y-2">
                {["OWASP Top 10 for Agentic Applications 2026", "ASI01, Agent Goal Hijack", "NIS-2, Art. 23 Incident Reporting"].map((r) => (
                  <p class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-1">{r}</p>
                ))}
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="border border-line-0 bg-bg-1 p-6">
              <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-1 mb-4">Key stat</p>
              <p class="font-mono text-sm text-ink-0 font-bold">48%</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-ink-1 mt-1">of security professionals in a Dark Reading readership poll ranked agentic AI as the top attack vector for 2026</p>
            </div>
            <div class="border border-line-0 bg-bg-1 p-6">
              <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-1 mb-3">Related</p>
              <div class="space-y-4">
                <a href="/blog/compromised-agent-no-logs" class="group flex items-start gap-2 text-xs text-ink-1 hover:text-ink-1 transition-colors leading-relaxed">
                  <span class="font-mono text-warn shrink-0 mt-0.5 group-hover:translate-x-0.5 transition-transform">→</span>
                  <span>What Happens When an AI Agent Gets Compromised, And Nobody Has the Logs</span>
                </a>
                <a href="/blog/shadow-ai-production-systems" class="group flex items-start gap-2 text-xs text-ink-1 hover:text-ink-1 transition-colors leading-relaxed">
                  <span class="font-mono text-warn shrink-0 mt-0.5 group-hover:translate-x-0.5 transition-transform">→</span>
                  <span>Shadow AI Is Already in Your Production Systems</span>
                </a>
              </div>
            </div>
          </aside>

        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

